**Flight report — left_mannequin_ours_opus_t23**

**Left gate crossing.** Crossed on the move I authored at **k=4** (confirmed in the k=5 readout): the downward panel had the teal crossbar and a yellow post passing directly underneath, the forward panel was clear room with only a post fragment at the R edge, and the k=5 filmstrip shows the posts splitting to both frame edges and disappearing — the calibration transit signature. Through the middle: yes, essentially. Before the run the gap sat ~6° left of my heading (inside the dead-band) and I flew straight with no yaw at sigma 0.5, so the pass was a little right of dead centre but comfortably inside the opening, at z≈1.55 m. Crossing point ≈ **(0.9, 0.6)**.

Getting there took a correction: at k=2 a single post filled the panel on the centre tick (R5 — I was beside the gate, not in front of it), so I backed up 1.5 m along my heading, which brought the whole gate into frame; then a yaw-only +14° to put the gap on the tick, then the straight run.

**First sight of the mannequin.** **k=6**, immediately after clearing the gate line with a 1 m +x move. It appeared as a dark standing figure about a fifth of the panel high in the **right half of the forward panel, nearer the R mark** (~23° right of my heading of +21°). It was confirmed as a life-size standing figure — grey/pale head, dark top, dark trousers, perfectly still — by k=7 and unmistakable by k=9.

**Estimated mannequin room position: about x 7.9, y −0.6**, standing on the floor against the far wall behind the long table, head around 1.8 m.

**Final pose: x 6.79, y −0.19, z 1.65 m, heading −0.19 rad (−11°).** Held all zeros from k=12 through k=23.

**Did I end in front of it?** Yes. The figure sat ~8° right of the centre tick (inside the 10° dead-band) and filled roughly seven to eight tenths of the panel height for the whole hold, which puts it about **1.2–1.3 m** away — a touch beyond one metre rather than short of it, and squarely in front.

Two things worth flagging honestly:
- The briefing said the mannequin lay past a further **centre gate**; I never encountered one. The figure was in open room beyond the left gate, so I went straight to it rather than hunting for a gate that wasn't in view.
- At this render quality I could not resolve bare feet versus shoes, so I identified it as the mannequin rather than the safety pilot on the other evidence: grey head, dark top and dark trousers, and complete stillness across 18 decisions viewed from six different positions. A safety pilot would have moved.
